---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import ScreenshotDialog from '../ScreenshotDialog.astro';
import homeClassic from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/home-classic.webp';
import homeBubbly from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/home-bubbly.webp';
import homeFlat from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/home-flat.webp';
import homeCurrent from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/home-current.webp';

// Historical provenance: Classic 5b483fdd, Bubbly 1d728caf, Flat ab713495.
// Current uses Cubby main; 2dc9a4a0 is the first Needs You + desktop-tabs baseline.
const homeScreenshots = [
	{
		full: homeClassic,
		alt: 'Classic Cubby desktop Home with stacked Tasks, Limits and Expiry in the old operational dashboard.',
		caption: 'Classic — one long operational Home',
	},
	{
		full: homeBubbly,
		alt: 'Bubbly Cubby desktop Home with a green Daily Life chapter and yellow Household chapter containing white cards.',
		caption: 'Bubbly — colored chapter containers',
	},
	{
		full: homeFlat,
		alt: 'Flat Cubby desktop Home with the colorless Wellbeing and Family areas, including Gym and Coffee among the longer surface.',
		caption: 'Flat — cleaner, but harder to find things',
	},
	{
		full: homeCurrent,
		alt: 'Current Cubby desktop Home with Needs You cards and predictable category tabs.',
		caption: 'Current — Needs You and category tabs',
	},
];
---

<section class="home-evolution border-neutral/15 border-t">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-16 sm:pb-24">
		<header class="home-prose">
			<h2 class="min-w-0 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Getting Home right</h2>
			<div class="text-base-content/85 grid min-w-0 gap-4 leading-relaxed">
				<p>
					Home kept getting harder because Cubby kept getting more useful. I wanted the things I did every day within
					roughly one scroll and one tap, even if that made the information architecture less neat. But every useful
					thing I kept on Home made the next one harder to fit.
				</p>
				<p>
					This wasn't a neat redesign from A to B. More than once I thought I'd finally made Home better, then spent the
					next few days using it and found something else annoying. Some of the choices were also hard to let go because
					they looked like what a "proper" app was supposed to do.
				</p>
			</div>
		</header>

		<section class="mt-16 sm:mt-20" aria-labelledby="home-tensions-title">
			<h3 id="home-tensions-title" class="max-w-md text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">
				What kept pulling Home in different directions
			</h3>
			<dl class="home-tensions mt-7">
				<div class="home-tension">
					<dt class="font-semibold">One stop or progressive disclosure?</dt>
					<dd class="text-base-content/70 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						A clean summary is less useful if I have to leave it every time I log a Task or add to a Limit. Those
						one-click actions were some of the most-used parts of Cubby. Keeping them on Home was clearly worth it; the
						harder question was what else deserved to sit beside them.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div class="home-tension">
					<dt class="font-semibold">One responsive Home or two compositions?</dt>
					<dd class="text-base-content/70 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						For a long time mobile and desktop showed the same things in different arrangements. Eventually I was using
						the phone for now, next and quick logging, while desktop was better for overview and browsing.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div class="home-tension">
					<dt class="font-semibold">Personality or cleanliness?</dt>
					<dd class="text-base-content/70 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						I wanted Cubby to feel warm and a little playful, not like an admin panel. But pastel sections full of white
						cards started to look like a design trope I was following because every app seemed to do it.
					</dd>
				</div>
			</dl>
		</section>

		<header class="home-prose mt-20 sm:mt-28">
			<h3 class="min-w-0 text-2xl font-bold lg:text-3xl">Four answers to the same problem</h3>
			<p class="text-base-content/75 min-w-0 leading-relaxed">
				The versions below weren't a march towards an obvious final design. Each one leaned harder into a different part
				of those trade-offs, then exposed a different problem.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="home-stages mt-8" data-screenshot-gallery data-gallery-dialog-id="cubby-home-evolution-dialog">
			<article class="home-stage">
				<button
					type="button"
					data-gallery-open="0"
					data-gallery-caption={homeScreenshots[0].caption}
					class="home-shot group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 focus-visible:outline-base-content/70 bg-base-200 active:bg-base-300 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4"
					aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${homeScreenshots[0].caption}`}>
					<Image
						src={homeScreenshots[0].full}
						alt={homeScreenshots[0].alt}
						loading="lazy"
						width={1200}
						densities={[1, 2]}
						class="block h-auto w-full"
					/>
					<span
						class="absolute right-2 bottom-2 grid size-9 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-85 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
						aria-hidden="true">
						<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
					</span>
				</button>
				<div class="home-stage-copy">
					<p class="cubby-accent text-sm font-semibold">#1 One long page</p>
					<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Put the everyday stuff in front of me</h4>
					<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
						The late classic dashboard was still one long operational page. Tasks and Limits sat near the top because I
						used their one-click logs constantly; Expiry, Enrichment, Illness, Finance and Jobs followed further down,
						with the quieter stuff collected at the end. The argument was simple: I shouldn't need to open a feature
						page for something I did several times a day.
					</p>
				</div>
			</article>

			<article class="home-stage">
				<button
					type="button"
					data-gallery-open="1"
					data-gallery-caption={homeScreenshots[1].caption}
					class="home-shot group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 focus-visible:outline-base-content/70 bg-base-200 active:bg-base-300 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4"
					aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${homeScreenshots[1].caption}`}>
					<Image
						src={homeScreenshots[1].full}
						alt={homeScreenshots[1].alt}
						loading="lazy"
						width={1200}
						densities={[1, 2]}
						class="block h-auto w-full"
					/>
					<span
						class="absolute right-2 bottom-2 grid size-9 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-85 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
						aria-hidden="true">
						<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
					</span>
				</button>
				<div class="home-stage-copy">
					<p class="cubby-accent text-sm font-semibold">#2 Bubbly containers</p>
					<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Make the dashboard feel like an app</h4>
					<div class="text-base-content/75 mt-4 grid gap-3 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I really thought this was better. The big soft-color sections and bubbly cards looked like the kind of
							Material-ish, flat pastel treatment I kept seeing in other apps. It finally felt less like a plain list.
						</p>
						<p>
							Using it was less convincing. Tasks and Limits were fine because I used their one-click logs all the time;
							they earned the space. Illness took a bit more finding, but when someone was sick I had a reason to go
							looking for the extra context. Jobs was easy to spot when there was a photo. Coffee and Gym were hopeless.
							Screen Timer wasn't much better. I kept scrolling around looking for them in the middle, and I couldn't
							remember which color was supposed to mean what.
						</p>
						<p>
							Worse, I didn't actually like how many white cards were sitting on pastel containers. I still wanted to
							make it work because it looked like what every app seemed to do.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</article>

			<article class="home-stage">
				<button
					type="button"
					data-gallery-open="2"
					data-gallery-caption={homeScreenshots[2].caption}
					class="home-shot group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 focus-visible:outline-base-content/70 bg-base-200 active:bg-base-300 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4"
					aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${homeScreenshots[2].caption}`}>
					<Image
						src={homeScreenshots[2].full}
						alt={homeScreenshots[2].alt}
						loading="lazy"
						width={1200}
						densities={[1, 2]}
						class="block h-auto w-full"
					/>
					<span
						class="absolute right-2 bottom-2 grid size-9 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-85 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
						aria-hidden="true">
						<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
					</span>
				</button>
				<div class="home-stage-copy">
					<p class="cubby-accent text-sm font-semibold">#3 Flat again</p>
					<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Containers became part of the noise</h4>
					<div class="text-base-content/75 mt-4 grid gap-3 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							So I went back the other way. I stripped out the category backgrounds, borders, rounded corners, and
							shadows and let spacing, headings and illustrations do more of the work.
						</p>
						<p>
							It was cleaner, but now Home was just a long list again. Coffee and Gym were technically right there, but
							that didn't mean I could find them quickly. On a phone, scroll position was at least a rough landmark.
							Desktop was worse: much more was visible at once, so everything was just in my face.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</article>

			<article class="home-stage">
				<button
					type="button"
					data-gallery-open="3"
					data-gallery-caption={homeScreenshots[3].caption}
					class="home-shot group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 focus-visible:outline-base-content/70 bg-base-200 active:bg-base-300 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4"
					aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${homeScreenshots[3].caption}`}>
					<Image
						src={homeScreenshots[3].full}
						alt={homeScreenshots[3].alt}
						loading="lazy"
						width={1200}
						densities={[1, 2]}
						class="block h-auto w-full"
					/>
					<span
						class="absolute right-2 bottom-2 grid size-9 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-85 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
						aria-hidden="true">
						<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
					</span>
				</button>
				<div class="home-stage-copy">
					<p class="cubby-accent text-sm font-semibold">#4 Different compositions</p>
					<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Stop making one Home do both jobs</h4>
					<div class="text-base-content/75 mt-4 grid gap-3 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I'd resisted tabs because they felt very SaaS-dashboard to me, and I liked the idea that Home was one
							place I could simply scroll through. But once I took the color containers away, the weakness of that
							approach was hard to ignore.
						</p>
						<p>
							So desktop now starts with Needs You and puts the rest behind predictable category tabs. Mobile went a
							different way again, with its own Today, Recent and Apps composition. That was the bigger change: stop
							pretending the same Home just needed better breakpoints.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</article>

			<ScreenshotDialog id="cubby-home-evolution" images={homeScreenshots} />
		</div>

		<footer class="home-prose home-afterword mt-20 border-t pt-8 sm:mt-28 sm:pt-10">
			<h3 class="min-w-0 text-2xl font-bold">Where it has landed for now</h3>
			<div class="text-base-content/85 grid min-w-0 gap-4 leading-relaxed">
				<p>
					I still don't want a perfectly tidy Home if tidy means making the common things slower. That was why I kept
					trying to make the one-stop dashboard work. But I also don't need every useful feature visible at once. Needs
					You is the compromise: put the few things that deserve attention in front of me, and keep the rest somewhere
					predictable.
				</p>
				<p>
					Splitting the compositions also gives mobile and desktop different visual budgets. I can keep some of the
					color and personality without asking every domain to shout at once. The data and actions underneath are still
					shared; what changes is what each screen puts in front of me.
				</p>
			</div>
		</footer>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	/* Hallmark · genre: editorial · tone: restrained · palette: warm cream + forest · macrostructure: Photographic · theme: incumbent Cubby · enrichment: supplied screenshots · pre-emit critique: P5 H5 E5 S5 R5 V5 · contrast: pass (40–41) · nav: incumbent · footer: incumbent · slop: pass (42–45) · honest: pass (46) · chrome: pass (47) · tokens: pass (48) · responsive: pass (49) · icons: pass (30) · mobile: pass (34, 49, 50–57) */
	.home-evolution :where(h2, h3, h4) {
		min-width: 0;
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}

	.home-prose {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1.5rem;
		max-width: 72ch;
	}

	.home-tension {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		padding-block: 1.25rem;
		border-block-start: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-base-content) 15%, transparent);
	}

	.home-stage + .home-stage {
		margin-block-start: clamp(4rem, 10vw, 7rem);
	}

	.home-stage-copy {
		max-width: 72ch;
		margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
	}

	.home-afterword {
		border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-base-content) 20%, transparent);
	}

	@media (min-width: 48rem) {
		.home-tension {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
			gap: 3rem;
		}
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.home-shot span {
			transition: none;
		}
	}
</style>
